-Knowing how people move in a multimodal transport system is a prerequisite for planning traffic and for designing networks that work. The difficulty is not only to reproduce travel patterns. It is also to build a simulation framework in which individual behaviour can be studied, behavioural hypotheses can be tested, and strategies for multimodal transport and urban traffic planning can be improved. For such a framework to inform a decision, its population must moreover reproduce, at least in aggregate, the travel behaviour recorded by the household travel surveys that planning authorities certify and publish (Tisséo Collectivités & AUAT, 2023). Mode choice, the split between car, public transport, walking and cycling, is the quantity on which this requirement bites hardest: it is what planners compare against survey targets, and what their policies aim to move.
+Understanding individual mobility behaviour in multimodal transport systems is essential to advance travel planning and to design efficient networks. The challenge is not only to reproduce aggregate flows: it is to build a simulation framework able to support the study of individual behaviour, the testing of fine-grained behavioural hypotheses and the design of more sustainable, personalised mobility policies. For this, the simulated population must also reproduce, at least in aggregate, the behaviour recorded by certified household travel surveys (Tisséo Collectivités & AUAT, 2023).
 
-Transport systems are becoming personalised and multimodal. This is part of a wider move toward mobility centred on the user, in which public transit, walking, cycling and shared services are combined into journeys that fit individual preferences (Smith et al., 1995; Horni, Nagel & Axhausen, 2016). Simulating such systems is hard, because a person's mobility choices depend on their socioeconomic profile, on the accessibility constraints they face and on what they have experienced (Grignard et al., 2018). Oberoi (2024) makes the point for Mobility-as-a-Service: personalising the service means dealing with the heterogeneity of users, their preferences, their contexts and their accessibility needs, and this remains a major obstacle to realistic transport models. A model therefore has to be flexible enough to represent heterogeneous, adaptive travel decisions, and realistic enough for those decisions to hold at the scale of a population.
+The rise of personalised multimodal transport systems reflects a transition toward user-centric mobility, in which public transit, walking, cycling and shared services are combined to offer journeys tailored to individual preferences (Smith et al., 1995; Horni, Nagel & Axhausen, 2016). Representing such systems in simulation is particularly complex, because mobility choices are shaped by heterogeneous socioeconomic profiles, accessibility constraints and lived experiences (Grignard et al., 2018). As Oberoi (2024) underlines, personalisation within Mobility-as-a-Service requires dealing with the heterogeneity of users, their preferences, their life contexts and their accessibility needs, which remains a major obstacle to realistic transport models. Modelling must therefore combine flexibility and realism to capture heterogeneous, adaptive travel decisions at population scale.
 
-Current transport simulations cope poorly with this complexity, and poorly with the data they need for calibration. Detailed trajectory and itinerary datasets, which are what reveals the fine structure of multimodal travel behaviour, are very rare (Fourez et al., 2025). They exist for a few large metropolitan areas, Tokyo being one example, and are missing almost everywhere else (Feng et al., 2024). Because calibration depends on such data, existing models transfer badly to smaller or less data-rich regions. And combining several external data sources demands heavy rule engineering, which yields brittle models, hard to scale and liable to fail when conditions were not foreseen.
+Current transport simulations struggle with this complexity and suffer from a shortage of calibration data: agent-based models require extensive local data, which hinders their adoption (Liu, Yang & Yin, 2024). Detailed multimodal trajectory and itinerary datasets, indispensable to grasp behavioural nuances, are extremely scarce (Fourez et al., 2025). They exist only for a restricted number of large metropolitan areas, Tokyo for instance, and their coverage is very uneven from one city to another (Feng et al., 2024). This dependence on unobtainable calibration data restricts the reach and the transferability of existing models. Moreover, integrating multiple external sources imposes heavy manual rule engineering, producing rigid models with fixed heuristics, costly to scale and helpless in unforeseen conditions (Liu, Yang & Yin, 2024; Alves et al., 2026).
 
-Over the last three years, large language models (LLMs) have entered agent-based simulation as decision engines. Generative agents endowed with natural-language reasoning, episodic memory and planning were introduced by Park et al. (2023). They have since been proposed for travel demand modelling (Liu et al., 2024), for large-scale urban simulation (Bougie & Watanabe, 2025), and for route replanning under road disruptions on the GAMA platform (Alves et al., 2026). The present work builds on the software architecture of Vu, Gaudou & Oberoi (2025), which couples GAMA (Taillandier et al., 2019) with an OpenTripPlanner routing service and an LLM decision module, and applies it to the Toulouse metropolitan area.
+Over the last three years, large language models (LLMs) have entered agent-based simulation as decision engines. Generative agents endowed with natural-language reasoning, episodic memory and planning were introduced by Park et al. (2023). They have since been proposed for travel demand modelling (Liu et al., 2024), for large-scale urban simulation (Bougie & Watanabe, 2025), and for route replanning under road disruptions on the GAMA platform (Alves et al., 2026). The present work is the continuation of the software architecture of Vu, Gaudou & Oberoi (2025), which couples GAMA (Taillandier et al., 2019) with an OpenTripPlanner routing service and an LLM decision module, and applies it to the Toulouse metropolitan area.
 
